.TE
.PP
They rely on RFC 793 behaviour.
Windows and some network devices reset every such probe, so every port
looks closed.
Most systems, Linux included, also reset Maimon probes to open ports, so
\f[V]-sM\f[R] shows them closed.
.SS ACK scan (\f[V]-sA\f[R])
.PP
Sends a bare ACK.
A reset means \f[V]unfiltered\f[R] (the probe got through a filter), no
answer or an ICMP error means \f[V]filtered\f[R].
It maps firewall rules; it cannot tell open from closed.
.SS Window scan (\f[V]-sW\f[R])
.PP
Sends an ACK like \f[V]-sA\f[R], and reads the TCP window of the reset:
non-zero means \f[V]open\f[R], zero means \f[V]closed\f[R]; no answer
means \f[V]filtered\f[R].
Only some systems put a non-zero window in such resets.
Linux always sends zero, so \f[V]-sW\f[R] shows every port of a Linux
host as closed.
.SS Custom TCP flags (\f[V]--scanflags <flags>\f[R])
.PP
Sends any combination of TCP flags instead of the scan\[aq]s own.
Flags are given as names run together, in any order (\f[V]SYNFIN\f[R],
\f[V]URGPSHFIN\f[R]; the names are \f[V]URG\f[R], \f[V]ACK\f[R],
\f[V]PSH\f[R], \f[V]RST\f[R], \f[V]SYN\f[R], \f[V]FIN\f[R],
\f[V]ECE\f[R], \f[V]CWR\f[R]), or as a number (\f[V]--scanflags 3\f[R]).
The answers are read as the scan type given with it reads them
(\f[V]-sA --scanflags ACKPSH\f[R] is read like an ACK scan), or as a SYN
scan\[aq]s when no TCP scan type is given.
It needs raw sockets, so it cannot be combined with \f[V]-sT\f[R] or
\f[V]-sn\f[R].
.SS UDP scan (\f[V]-sU\f[R])
.PP
Sends a datagram to each port.
A UDP reply means \f[V]open\f[R], an ICMP port unreachable
\f[V]closed\f[R], another ICMP unreachable error \f[V]filtered\f[R], and
no answer \f[V]open|filtered\f[R].
Works without privileges.
.PP
Well-known ports get a payload their service answers: DNS (53, 5353),
NTP (123), SNMP (161) and SSDP (1900).
Every other port gets the payload \f[V]FeroProbeScan/1\[rs]n\f[R] (see
Probe signature).
.PP
Hosts rate-limit their ICMP errors, so a fast scan of many closed UDP
ports can show some of them as \f[V]open|filtered\f[R].
Lower \f[V]--concurrency\f[R] or add a \f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] for more
accurate results (\f[V]--max-rate\f[R] does not pace UDP probes).
.SS SCTP INIT scan (\f[V]-sY\f[R])
.PP
SCTP carries telephone- and mobile-network signalling (SIGTRAN,
Diameter, S1AP/NGAP).
\f[V]-sY\f[R] sends an INIT chunk, the first step of SCTP\[aq]s
handshake, and never completes it: an INIT-ACK means \f[V]open\f[R], an
ABORT \f[V]closed\f[R], and no answer or an ICMP unreachable error
\f[V]filtered\f[R].
Name SCTP ports with \f[V]S:\f[R] in \f[V]-p\f[R]; without \f[V]-p\f[R]
a built-in list of common SCTP ports is scanned.
FeroProbe does not check the CRC32c checksum of SCTP replies.
.SS SCTP COOKIE ECHO scan (\f[V]-sZ\f[R])
.PP
Sends a COOKIE ECHO chunk out of the blue.
An open port drops it silently (\f[V]open|filtered\f[R]), and a closed
one answers with an ABORT (\f[V]closed\f[R]).
It gets past some firewalls that block INIT chunks, but cannot tell open
from filtered.
\f[V]-sY\f[R] and \f[V]-sZ\f[R] cannot run together.
.SS IP protocol scan (\f[V]-sO\f[R])
.PP
Finds which IP protocols (ICMP, IGMP, TCP, UDP, GRE, ESP, SCTP, ...)
a host supports.
Its \[dq]ports\[dq] are protocol numbers 0 to 255, named with
\f[V]P:\f[R] in \f[V]-p\f[R] (\f[V]-sO -p P:1,6,17,47\f[R]); without
\f[V]-p\f[R] all 256 are tried.
ICMP, IGMP, TCP, UDP and SCTP get a real probe (an echo request, a
membership query, an ACK, a datagram, an INIT); every other protocol is
sent as a bare IP header.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
