SCTP ports
T}
T{
\f[V]P:\f[R]
T}@T{
IP protocol numbers (0-255) for \f[V]-sO\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
Values before any prefix apply to every scan that runs:
\f[V]-sS -sU -p 22,U:53,T:80\f[R] scans TCP 22 and 80 and UDP 22 and 53.
FeroProbe warns, like nmap, when the prefixes do not match the scans
(\f[V]-sS -p U:53\f[R] warns that \f[V]U:\f[R] needs \f[V]-sU\f[R]), and
a scan type left with no ports is skipped with a warning rather than
failing.
Cannot be combined with \f[V]--top-ports\f[R] or \f[V]-F\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--top-ports <n>\f[R]
.PP
Scan the \f[V]n\f[R] most common ports of each protocol scanned.
Without it the defaults above apply.
.SS \f[V]-F\f[R] (fast mode)
.PP
Scan the 100 most common ports of each protocol scanned.
Cannot be combined with \f[V]-p\f[R] or \f[V]--top-ports\f[R].
.SS \f[V]--exclude-ports <port ranges>\f[R]
.PP
Leave these ports out of the scan.
Written like \f[V]-p\f[R], prefixes included; with \f[V]-sO\f[R],
\f[V]P:\f[R] numbers are left out of the protocol list.
As in nmap, the excluded ports are also removed from the discovery
probes (see Host discovery).
.SS \f[V]-r\f[R] (don\[aq]t randomize ports)
.PP
Probe ports in numerical order.
By default the order is shuffled, as in nmap.
Reports are always sorted.
.SH SERVICE AND VERSION DETECTION
.SS \f[V]-sV\f[R]
.PP
After the port scan, find out which service and version run on each open
port.
It runs on the hosts found up, together with any TCP scan (the default
one if none was given) and \f[V]-sU\f[R].
Cannot be combined with \f[V]-sn\f[R].
It works without privileges.
.PP
For each open TCP port, FeroProbe:
.IP "1." 3
Tries TLS first on the usual TLS ports (443, 993, 8443 and similar), and
probes the service inside the tunnel.
The certificate\[aq]s subject name is shown with the version
(\f[V]cert CN: example.org\f[R]).
.IP "2." 3
Sends the probes meant for that port: HTTP, Redis, memcached,
PostgreSQL, AMQP, MQTT, RDP (with its security mode), Microsoft SQL
Server (with its release), SMB (dialect and signing), LDAP (vendor, host
and naming context), MongoDB, ZooKeeper, RTSP and SIP.
.IP "3." 3
Listens, since SSH, FTP, SMTP, POP3, IMAP, MySQL, VNC, rsync, NNTP and
IRC speak first.
.IP "4." 3
If the service stays silent, tries HTTP and then TLS.
.PP
Open UDP ports get a DNS \f[V]version.bind\f[R] query, an NTP query or
an SNMP query where they fit.
.PP
Replies are matched against FeroProbe\[aq]s own rules, in
\f[V]crates/feroprobe-core/src/service/rules.rs\f[R]; nmap\[aq]s probe
database is not used, because its licence does not allow redistributing
it.
The SERVICE column then shows the service (\f[V]ssl/http\f[R] for HTTP
inside TLS) and a VERSION column is added.
A reply no rule recognizes is printed under \[dq]Unrecognized service
replies\[dq] in normal output, which is the starting point for writing a
new rule.
.SS \f[V]--version-timeout <ms>\f[R]
.PP
How long to wait for each service reply, in milliseconds.
Default 3000.
The timing templates do not change it.
.SH OS DETECTION
.SS \f[V]-O\f[R]
.PP
Guess each host\[aq]s operating system family.
FeroProbe sends one TCP SYN that offers every common TCP option to an
open port, and reads the SYN-ACK: the initial TTL (64, 128 or 255; for
IPv6 the hop limit), the order of the TCP options that come back, the
\[dq]don\[aq]t fragment\[dq] bit and the window.
The TTL also gives the network distance in hops.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
OS guess: Linux 3.x - 6.x (90%), from 22/tcp
OS fingerprint: ttl=64 df=1 win=65483 opts=M,S,T,N,W mss=65495 ws=7
Network distance: 1 hop
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The signatures are FeroProbe\[aq]s own, in
\f[V]crates/feroprobe-core/src/os.rs\f[R], not nmap\[aq]s database, so
the guess names a family (Linux, Windows, macOS, FreeBSD, OpenBSD,
network devices) rather than an exact version.
The fingerprint line shows the raw traits, from which new signatures are
written.
IPv4 and IPv6 hosts are both covered; IPv6 replies are read from a
packet capture so that the hop limit is visible.
.PP
\f[V]-O\f[R] needs raw sockets (Linux, root or \f[V]CAP_NET_RAW\f[R])
and a port scan that finds at least one open TCP port; it is an error
without raw sockets and cannot be combined with \f[V]-sn\f[R].
If no port scan is left to run (every scan type lost its ports), OS
detection is skipped with a warning.
Its probes deliberately carry no FeroProbe signature, since they must
look like a real operating system\[aq]s.
.SH TRACEROUTE AND AGGRESSIVE MODE
.SS \f[V]--traceroute\f[R]
.PP
After the port scan, trace the route to each host that is up.
FeroProbe sends TCP SYN probes with TTL (IPv6: hop limit) 1 to 30 all at
once, to a TCP port the scan found open on that host, else one it found
closed, else a fallback port the command already probes: the first port
of the TCP scan; with \f[V]-sn\f[R], the first TCP port given with
\f[V]-p\f[R]; else the first TCP port of host discovery (\f[V]-PS\f[R],
\f[V]-PA\f[R], or the default 443 as root).
Traceroute never probes a port outside the scan: when the command probes
no TCP port at all (for example \f[V]-sU -Pn\f[R]), hosts without an
open or closed TCP port are not traced and a warning says why, and
\f[V]-sn -Pn --traceroute\f[R] without \f[V]-p\f[R] is refused.
Routers answer the probes that expire with ICMP time exceeded, and the
first probe that reaches the host ends the route.
A hop that never answers is shown as \f[V]...\f[R].
Needs raw sockets.
With \f[V]-Pn\f[R] every target is traced, even one that never answered.
Hosts given up on by \f[V]--host-timeout\f[R] are not traced.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
TRACEROUTE (using port 443/tcp)
HOP RTT        ADDRESS
1   0.29 ms    192.168.1.1
2   ...
3   9.84 ms    203.0.113.5
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
To trace without a port scan, combine it with \f[V]-sn\f[R], and with
\f[V]-sn -Pn\f[R] to skip host discovery too:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
sudo feroprobe -sn --traceroute example.org
sudo feroprobe -sn -Pn --traceroute -p 443 example.org
\f[R]
.fi
.SS \f[V]-A\f[R] (aggressive detection)
.PP
The same as \f[V]-sV -O --traceroute\f[R].
Without raw sockets only \f[V]-sV\f[R] runs, rather than failing, as in
nmap.
Unlike nmap, which drops the other parts silently, FeroProbe prints a
warning naming them:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Warning: -A without root or CAP_NET_RAW skips OS detection (-O) and traceroute (--traceroute), which send raw packets; only version detection (-sV) runs. Run as root, or grant CAP_NET_RAW, to include them
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
nmap\[aq]s \f[V]-A\f[R] also runs its default scripts (\f[V]-sC\f[R]);
FeroProbe has no scripts.
Cannot be combined with \f[V]-sn\f[R].
.SH TIMING AND PERFORMANCE
.SS Timing templates (\f[V]-T<0-5>\f[R])
.PP
\f[V]-T0\f[R] to \f[V]-T5\f[R], or \f[V]-T\f[R] with a name
(\f[V]-T paranoid\f[R] ...
\f[V]-T insane\f[R]), pick a bundle of timing settings, as in nmap.
\f[V]-T3\f[R] (normal) is the default.
Any of \f[V]--timeout\f[R], \f[V]--retries\f[R],
\f[V]-c\f[R]/\f[V]--concurrency\f[R], \f[V]--max-rate\f[R] and
\f[V]--scan-delay\f[R] given explicitly overrides the template\[aq]s
value for that setting, so \f[V]-T4 --max-rate 2000\f[R] is aggressive
with a lower packet rate.
.PP
