Any scan
T}@T{
Adaptive timeouts and up to 10 resends, instead of a fixed
\f[V]--timeout\f[R] and \f[V]--retries\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
The run ends with the same reminder.
These come from nmap 7.99\[aq]s source.
.PP
A dry run needs no root, writes no report files (it warns if
\f[V]-oN\f[R], \f[V]-oX\f[R], \f[V]-oG\f[R], \f[V]-oJ\f[R] or
\f[V]-oA\f[R] were given), and notes when a real run of the same command
would need raw-socket privileges.
The evasion address check still applies: a public address with
\f[V]-f\f[R], \f[V]--mtu\f[R], \f[V]-D\f[R] or \f[V]-S\f[R] is refused
unless \f[V]--allow-public-evasion\f[R] is given.
.PP
This matters most on operational-technology and industrial-control
(OT/ICS) networks, where an unexpected packet can hang or reset a
fragile device such as a PLC or RTU.
With \f[V]--dry-run\f[R], an operator can check every target, port, flag
and fragment a command would send, and confirm it stays within the
approved scope, before any scan probe is sent.
Then run the same command without \f[V]--dry-run\f[R], ideally with a
gentle timing template (\f[V]-T0\f[R] or \f[V]-T1\f[R]), a low
\f[V]--max-rate\f[R] and a short, explicit port list, with FeroProbe:
the same command run with nmap sends other probes.
The closest nmap option, the list scan \f[V]-sL\f[R], only lists the
targets.
.SH MISCELLANEOUS OPTIONS
.SS \f[V]-6\f[R]
.PP
Use the IPv6 address of hostname targets; see Target specification.
.SS \f[V]-A\f[R]
.PP
Version detection, OS detection and traceroute; see Traceroute and
aggressive mode.
.SS \f[V]--override-ics-protection\f[R]
.PP
Run once despite ICS protection, allowing \f[V]-sV\f[R], \f[V]-O\f[R]
and \f[V]-A\f[R], with a prominent warning naming what it allowed.
Changes no settings file.
See Settings files and ICS protection.
.SS \f[V]--oui-file <file>\f[R]
.PP
The IEEE OUI list used to name the vendor of MAC addresses found by ARP
or neighbour discovery.
FeroProbe reads \f[V]oui.txt\f[R], \f[V]oui.csv\f[R] and Wireshark
\f[V]manuf\f[R] files.
Without this option it uses the first of
\f[V]/usr/share/hwdata/oui.txt\f[R],
\f[V]/usr/share/ieee-data/oui.txt\f[R] and similar system locations that
exists (installed by the \f[V]hwdata\f[R] or \f[V]ieee-data\f[R]
packages, or by Wireshark), and otherwise a small built-in table of
common vendors.
The IEEE publishes the list at
\f[V]https://standards-oui.ieee.org/oui/oui.csv\f[R].
.SS \f[V]-V\f[R], \f[V]--version\f[R]
.PP
Print the version and exit.
.SS \f[V]-h\f[R], \f[V]--help\f[R]
.PP
Print the help page and exit.
Running \f[V]feroprobe\f[R] with no arguments does the same.
.SH SETTINGS FILES AND ICS PROTECTION
.PP
FeroProbe reads optional settings from TOML files when it starts, lowest
precedence first:
.IP "1." 3
\f[V]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/feroprobe/config.toml\f[R]
(\f[V]\[ti]/.config/feroprobe/config.toml\f[R] when
\f[V]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is unset or not absolute;
\f[V]%APPDATA%\[rs]feroprobe\[rs]config.toml\f[R] on Windows without
\f[V]HOME\f[R]);
.IP "2." 3
under \f[V]sudo\f[R], the same file in the invoking user\[aq]s home
(from \f[V]SUDO_USER\f[R]), since sudo points \f[V]HOME\f[R] at
root\[aq]s (skipped when sudo kept an absolute
\f[V]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R], which already names your file);
.IP "3." 3
\f[V].feroproberc\f[R] in the current directory.
.PP
A key in a later file replaces the same key from an earlier one, and
command-line options win over all of them.
A missing file is skipped; one that cannot be read, is not valid TOML or
gives a value of the wrong type stops the run.
Unknown keys only warn.
\f[V]-v\f[R] prints which files were read and whether ICS protection is
on.
nmap has no such files.
.PP
The one setting is \f[V]ics_protection\f[R], \f[B]off by default\f[R]:
FeroProbe is a general scanner and behaves like nmap unless you ask
otherwise.
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ics_protection = true
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
With it on:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-sV\f[R], \f[V]-O\f[R] and \f[V]-A\f[R] are refused before any name
is resolved or packet sent, with a message naming the option and where
protection was turned on.
Version detection sends application-protocol probes (HTTP, TLS, ...)
to every open port, and fragile PLCs and RTUs can fault or hang when,
for example, their Modbus handler on port 502 receives an HTTP request.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--override-ics-protection\f[R] lets a single run through with a
warning.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]--dry-run\f[R] is not refused, since it sends no probes, but warns
that the real run would be.
.IP \[bu] 2
A scan that includes a well-known OT port (102/tcp S7comm, 502/tcp
Modbus, 20000/tcp+udp DNP3, 44818/tcp+udp EtherNet/IP, 47808/udp BACnet)
prints a warning.
Ports are never refused.
.SS Hardened build
.PP
Building with the \f[V]ics-protection-default\f[R] Cargo feature makes
protection always on in that binary: settings files cannot switch it off
(a \f[V]ics_protection = false\f[R] is ignored with a warning), and only
\f[V]--override-ics-protection\f[R] allows a single run.
For teams that hand out a FeroProbe binary for use on control networks:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
cargo build --release -p feroprobe --features ics-protection-default
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
See docs/ics-protection.md for the full rationale.
.SH PRIVILEGES AND PLATFORMS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
